ntdll: bounds-check RtlIsEcCode so a wild pointer cannot become a stack overflow.

RtlIsEcCode indexes the PEB's EC code bitmap with no upper bound:

    const UINT64 *map = (const UINT64 *)NtCurrentTeb()->Peb->EcCodeBitMap;
    ULONG_PTR page = ptr / page_size;
    return (map[page / 64] >> (page & 63)) & 1;

The bitmap is allocated to cover exactly the user address space (alloc_arm64ec_map: one bit per page of
`address_space_limit`), so any pointer past the end of user space reads off the end of the mapping.

On its own that would be a benign-looking out-of-bounds read. It is not, because RtlIsEcCode runs while
an exception is being DISPATCHED (raise_func_trampoline / unwind, to decide whether a PC is EC code). A
fault inside it re-enters dispatch, which calls RtlIsEcCode again on the same wild value, and the thread
recurses until its stack is gone -- so one stray pointer anywhere in a process turns into an
unrecoverable STATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW instead of an access violation the application could have handled.

MEASURED with Red Dead Redemption 2 on Hangover 11.15-arm64ec under FEX. The game takes one access
violation in its own code (rip inside RDR2.exe, reading an unmapped page), and that single fault became:

    1  x c0000005 at rip=0000000146d1d06c   <- the real fault, in guest code
 1752  x c0000005 at rip=00006fffffa3a6dc   <- RtlIsEcCode+0x10, over and over
    1  x c00000fd (STATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW)   <- the thread finally dies

The wild value was 0x1cc00e24a0000, recovered while unwinding the guest stack; at a 16K page size that
indexes about 0.9 TB past the end of the bitmap. Raising the thread stack floor to 8 MB did NOT help --
the recursion simply consumed that too -- which is what identified it as unbounded recursion rather than
a stack that was merely too small.

With the check in place the same run produces zero access violations and no stack overflow, and the
guest's original fault is delivered normally. (RDR2 then stops for an unrelated reason, an SEH frame
pointer whose low half is missing -- see preliminaries/RESEARCH.md.)

The limit is queried once via SystemBasicInformation rather than hard-coded, so it tracks whatever the
process's user address space actually is.

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/signal_arm64ec.c b/dlls/ntdll/signal_arm64ec.c
index 0c7032f..33911af 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/signal_arm64ec.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/signal_arm64ec.c
@@ -1405,6 +1405,23 @@ __ASM_GLOBAL_FUNC( "#KiUserCallbackDispatcher",
                    "brk #1" )
 
 
+/* Highest user address, cached: the EC bitmap covers exactly this much address space. */
+static ULONG_PTR ec_map_limit(void)
+{
+    static ULONG_PTR limit;
+
+    if (!limit)
+    {
+        SYSTEM_BASIC_INFORMATION info;
+
+        if (!NtQuerySystemInformation( SystemBasicInformation, &info, sizeof(info), NULL ))
+            limit = (ULONG_PTR)info.HighestUserAddress;
+        else
+            limit = (ULONG_PTR)1 << 47;  /* conservative fallback */
+    }
+    return limit;
+}
+
 /**************************************************************************
  *              RtlIsEcCode (NTDLL.@)
  */
@@ -1412,6 +1429,15 @@ BOOLEAN WINAPI RtlIsEcCode( ULONG_PTR ptr )
 {
     const UINT64 *map = (const UINT64 *)NtCurrentTeb()->Peb->EcCodeBitMap;
     ULONG_PTR page = ptr / page_size;
+
+    /* The bitmap holds one bit per page of USER address space and nothing more, so an address past
+     * the end of user space indexes off the end of the mapping. That matters far more here than the
+     * bounds check might suggest: RtlIsEcCode is called while DISPATCHING an exception, so faulting
+     * inside it turns one wild pointer into an unrecoverable loop -- the fault re-enters dispatch,
+     * which calls RtlIsEcCode again, until the thread's stack is gone. Observed with RDR2: a single
+     * guest access violation became 1752 identical faults at RtlIsEcCode+0x10 and then
+     * STATUS_STACK_OVERFLOW, the wild value being 0x1cc00e24a0000 recovered while unwinding. */
+    if (ptr >= ec_map_limit()) return FALSE;
     return (map[page / 64] >> (page & 63)) & 1;
 }
 
